<section>
  <h2>5. Higher Order Thinking Skills &mdash; Question Bank</h2>

  <!-- ---------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <!--  QUESTION 1                                                -->
  <!-- ---------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <div class="question">
    <p class="q-header">Question 1 &mdash; Graphical Analysis of Experimental Error</p>
    <p class="q-context">A student investigates energy conservation in a mass&ndash;spring system using a motion sensor to measure displacement and velocity. The theoretical prediction is E<sub>k</sub> = &frac12;k(x<sub>0</sub>&sup2; &minus; x&sup2;). The experimental plot of E<sub>k</sub> against displacement x shows a systematic deviation: the experimental curve lies approximately 8% below the theoretical curve at all displacements, and the discrepancy is largest near x = 0. The student's data include error bars of &plusmn;4% on each E<sub>k</sub> measurement.</p>

    <div class="graph-container" style="max-width:550px;margin:0.6rem auto;">
      {{GRAPH_HOTS_EXPERIMENTAL}}
      <p class="caption">Fig. 6: Theoretical E<sub>k</sub> (dashed) and experimental data with error bars (solid). The experimental curve shows a systematic offset.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="q-part">
      <p><span class="cmd-word">(a) Describe</span> the difference between the theoretical and experimental E<sub>k</sub> curves shown in Fig. 5. Explain whether the discrepancy is within the experimental uncertainty. <span class="marks">[2]</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="q-part">
      <p><span class="cmd-word">(b) Suggest</span> one physical cause for the systematic energy loss evident in the data. <span class="marks">[2]</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="q-part">
      <p><span class="cmd-word">(c) Evaluate</span> how the total energy of the system would appear on the experimental graph compared to the constant theoretical value. Sketch the experimental E<sub>total</sub> &ndash; x graph in your answer. <span class="marks">[3]</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mark-scheme">
    <p class="ms-header">Mark Scheme &mdash; Question 1</p>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(a)</strong> M1: The experimental curve is consistently below the theoretical curve at all displacements (accept: systematic offset/shift). A1: The error bars (&plusmn;4%) are smaller than the systematic deviation (~8%) at most points, so the discrepancy cannot be attributed to random uncertainty alone &mdash; a systematic error is present. [2]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(b)</strong> M1: Energy loss due to damping (air resistance or internal friction in the spring). A1: Damping causes mechanical energy to be converted to thermal energy in the surroundings, reducing the kinetic energy at every displacement. [2]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(c)</strong> M1: The experimental E<sub>total</sub> would not be constant &mdash; it would decrease over time as energy is dissipated. A1: A sketch showing E<sub>total</sub> declining with each oscillation (step-like or monotonic decay). A1: The experimental E<sub>total</sub> would be calculated as E<sub>k</sub> + E<sub>p</sub>; since E<sub>k</sub> is systematically low, E<sub>total</sub> would appear to decrease below &frac12;kx<sub>0</sub>&sup2;. [3]</div>
    <p class="ms-total">Total: [7]</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ---------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <!--  QUESTION 2                                                -->
  <!-- ---------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <div class="question">
    <p class="q-header">Question 2 &mdash; Evaluating Experimental Limitations</p>
    <p class="q-context">A student uses a motion sensor (resolution &plusmn;0.2 cm) and a force sensor (resolution &plusmn;0.01 N) to investigate energy conservation in a mass&ndash;spring system with m = 0.200 &plusmn; 0.001 kg, angular frequency &omega; = 5.0 &plusmn; 0.1 rad s<sup>&minus;1</sup>, and amplitude x<sub>0</sub> = 5.0 &plusmn; 0.1 cm.</p>

    <div class="q-part">
      <p><span class="cmd-word">(a) Calculate</span> the percentage uncertainty in the kinetic energy E<sub>k</sub> at the equilibrium position (x = 0) due to the uncertainties in m and &omega; only. State the value of E<sub>k</sub> at equilibrium with its absolute uncertainty. <span class="marks">[3]</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="q-part">
      <p><span class="cmd-word">(b) Explain</span> why the apparent total energy measured by the student may appear to increase slightly near the turning points even when no external work is done on the system. <span class="marks">[2]</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="q-part">
      <p><span class="cmd-word">(c) Evaluate</span> whether a data logger sampling at 50 Hz can reliably measure the maximum kinetic energy for a system oscillating with period T = 0.80 s. Justify your reasoning with a calculation. <span class="marks">[2]</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mark-scheme">
    <p class="ms-header">Mark Scheme &mdash; Question 2</p>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(a)</strong> M1: At equilibrium, v = &omega;x<sub>0</sub> = 5.0 &times; 0.050 = 0.250 m s<sup>&minus;1</sup>. E<sub>k</sub> = &frac12;mv&sup2; = &frac12; &times; 0.200 &times; 0.250&sup2; = 6.25 mJ. For fractional uncertainty in v&sup2;: &Delta;v/v = &Delta;&omega;/&omega; + &Delta;x<sub>0</sub>/x<sub>0</sub> = 0.1/5.0 + 0.1/5.0 = 0.04, so &Delta;(v&sup2;)/v&sup2; = 2 &times; 0.04 = 0.08. A1: &Delta;E<sub>k</sub>/E<sub>k</sub> = &Delta;m/m + &Delta;(v&sup2;)/v&sup2; = 0.001/0.200 + 0.08 = 0.005 + 0.08 = 0.085 = 8.5%. A1: &Delta;E<sub>k</sub> = 0.085 &times; 6.25 = 0.53 mJ. E<sub>k</sub> = (6.25 &plusmn; 0.53) mJ. [3]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(b)</strong> M1: At turning points the velocity is near zero but the force sensor reading for displacement may lag due to the finite sampling rate. A1: If displacement and velocity are not measured simultaneously, E<sub>k</sub> might be assigned to the wrong x, making E<sub>total</sub> = E<sub>k</sub> + E<sub>p</sub> appear to exceed the initial total energy. [2]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(c)</strong> M1: The period T = 0.80 s gives frequency f = 1.25 Hz. The sampling frequency 50 Hz gives 50/1.25 = 40 samples per period. A1: With 40 samples per period the sensor will capture data at ~9&deg; intervals &mdash; this is sufficient to resolve the E<sub>k</sub> maximum to within ~1% error, so the measurement is reliable. [2]</div>
    <p class="ms-total">Total: [7]</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ---------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <!--  QUESTION 3                                                -->
  <!-- ---------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <div class="question">
    <p class="q-header">Question 3 &mdash; Predicting the Effect of Damping</p>
    <p class="q-context">A mass&ndash;spring system oscillates in a viscous fluid. The damping is light. The initial amplitude is x<sub>0</sub> and the initial total energy is E<sub>0</sub> = &frac12;kx<sub>0</sub>&sup2;.</p>

    <div class="q-part">
      <p><span class="cmd-word">(a) Sketch</span> the total energy E<sub>total</sub> against time t for both the undamped and the lightly damped case on the same axes. Label the initial energy E<sub>0</sub> and mark three successive maxima. <span class="marks">[3]</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="q-part">
      <p><span class="cmd-word">(b) Explain</span> why the amplitude of a lightly damped oscillator decays exponentially rather than linearly. Refer to the energy loss per cycle. <span class="marks">[2]</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="q-part">
      <p><span class="cmd-word">(c) Predict</span> how the resonance frequency of this system changes as the degree of damping increases. Justify your answer. <span class="marks">[2]</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mark-scheme">
    <p class="ms-header">Mark Scheme &mdash; Question 3</p>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(a)</strong> B1: Undamped: horizontal line at height E<sub>0</sub> (constant energy). B1: Damped: curve starting at E<sub>0</sub> and decaying with decreasing maxima. B1: Three successive maxima labelled, each lower than the previous, following an approximately exponential envelope. [3]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(b)</strong> M1: The energy lost per cycle is proportional to the energy stored (for light damping, dissipative force &prop; velocity). A1: This gives dE/dt &prop; &minus;E, leading to exponential decay E(t) = E<sub>0</sub>e<sup>&minus;&gamma;t</sup>. Since E &prop; A&sup2;, the amplitude decays as A(t) = A<sub>0</sub>e<sup>&minus;&gamma;t/2</sup>. [2]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(c)</strong> M1: The resonance frequency decreases slightly with increasing damping. A1: Damping reduces the effective restoring force and the oscillator responds more sluggishly; the resonance peak shifts to lower frequencies and becomes broader. [2]</div>
    <p class="ms-total">Total: [7]</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ---------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <!--  QUESTION 4                                                -->
  <!-- ---------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <div class="question">
    <p class="q-header">Question 4 &mdash; Comparing Energy in Different SHM Systems</p>
    <p class="q-context">Consider two oscillating systems: (i) a mass&ndash;spring system on a frictionless horizontal surface, and (ii) a simple pendulum of length L displaced through a small angle.</p>

    <div class="q-part">
      <p><span class="cmd-word">(a) State</span> the form(s) of energy present at maximum displacement and at the equilibrium position for each system. Tabulate your answer. <span class="marks">[2]</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="q-part">
      <p><span class="cmd-word">(b) Explain</span> why the simple pendulum is only approximately SHM for small angular displacements (&theta; &lt; 10&deg;). Refer to the restoring force in your explanation. <span class="marks">[2]</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="q-part">
      <p><span class="cmd-word">(c) Discuss</span> whether total mechanical energy is strictly conserved for a pendulum over many oscillations in a real (non-ideal) setting. What experimental evidence would you look for? <span class="marks">[3]</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mark-scheme">
    <p class="ms-header">Mark Scheme &mdash; Question 4</p>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(a)</strong> B1: Mass&ndash;spring: at max displacement &rarr; elastic potential energy only; at equilibrium &rarr; kinetic energy only. B1: Pendulum: at max displacement &rarr; gravitational potential energy only; at equilibrium &rarr; kinetic energy only. Table with correct entries. [2]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(b)</strong> M1: For a pendulum, the restoring force is F = &minus;mg sin&theta;. A1: For small &theta;, sin&theta; &asymp; &theta; (in radians), so F &asymp; &minus;mg&theta; = &minus;(mg/L)x, giving SHM. For larger &theta;, sin&theta; &lt; &theta; and the motion deviates. [2]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(c)</strong> M1: In a non-ideal setting, mechanical energy is not strictly conserved due to air resistance and friction at the pivot. B1: Evidence: amplitude gradually decreases over time (the pendulum slows and stops). B1: Also: energy is dissipated as thermal energy (slight warming of pivot and surrounding air), which could be measured with sensitive thermometry. [3]</div>
    <p class="ms-total">Total: [7]</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ---------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <!--  QUESTION 5                                                -->
  <!-- ---------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <div class="question">
    <p class="q-header">Question 5 &mdash; Data Evaluation and Determination of Parameters</p>
    <p class="q-context">A student records the kinetic energy E<sub>k</sub> of a 50 g mass oscillating on a spring at various displacements x from equilibrium. The data are shown in the table.</p>

    <table class="data-table">
      <tr>
